## Answer Leak Check

The problem text contains only the integral to evaluate — no solutions, no sketches, no Lean statements, no embedded answers. Clean.

## Solution

Evaluate
$$I = \iint_{x^2+y^2 \leq 1} \frac{(x-y)^2}{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy.$$

**Polar substitution.** Let $x = r\cos\theta$, $y = r\sin\theta$, so $x^2+y^2 = r^2$ and $dx\,dy = r\,dr\,d\theta$. The disk $x^2+y^2\le 1$ maps to $0\le r\le 1$, $0\le\theta\le 2\pi$.

**Simplify the integrand.**
$$(x-y)^2 = x^2-2xy+y^2 = r^2(\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta-2\sin\theta\cos\theta)=r^2(1-\sin 2\theta).$$
Hence
$$\frac{(x-y)^2}{x^2+y^2}=\frac{r^2(1-\sin 2\theta)}{r^2}=1-\sin 2\theta,$$
which is independent of $r$.

**Convert the integral.**
$$I = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 (1-\sin 2\theta)\,r\,dr\,d\theta
= \left(\int_0^{2\pi}(1-\sin 2\theta)\,d\theta\right)\!\left(\int_0^1 r\,dr\right).$$

**Evaluate each factor.**
$$\int_0^1 r\,dr = \frac{1}{2}.$$
$$\int_0^{2\pi}(1-\sin 2\theta)\,d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi}1\,d\theta - \int_0^{2\pi}\sin 2\theta\,d\theta = 2\pi - 0 = 2\pi,$$
since $\sin 2\theta$ has period $\pi$ and integrates to $0$ over any full period.

**Combine.**
$$I = 2\pi \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \pi.$$

$$\boxed{\pi}$$

